{
  "anomaly_detected": true,
  "confidence": 0.45,
  "anomalies": [
    {
      "category": "open_connection",
      "components": ["NAVC2"],
      "pins": ["NAVC2.18", "NAVC2.63", "NAVC2.12", "NAVC2.19", "NAVC2.32", "NAVC2.48", "NAVC2.64"],
      "nets": ["Net-(NAVC2-VSS-Pad18)", "unconnected-(NAVC2-VDD-Pad19)", "unconnected-(NAVC2-VSSA-Pad12)"],
      "observed": "The NAVC MCU (STM32F405RGTx) has no ground connection in the netlist. Its two VSS pins 18 and 63 sit on a private net 'Net-(NAVC2-VSS-Pad18)' that is not GND. VSSA (pin 12) and all four VDD pins (19, 32, 48, 64) are listed as unconnected. By contrast, the FCC2 VSS pins are on GND.",
      "expected": "All VSS and VSSA pins of the NAVC MCU should be on GND, and all VDD pins should be on the 3V3 supply, as they are for the FCC MCU's VSS pins.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "netlist", "quote": "(net (code \"144\") (name \"Net-(NAVC2-VSS-Pad18)\") (class \"Default\") (node (ref \"NAVC2\") (pin \"18\") (pinfunction \"VSS\") (pintype \"power_in\")) (node (ref \"NAVC2\") (pin \"63\") (pinfunction \"VSS\") (pintype \"passive\")))"},
        {"source": "netlist", "quote": "(net (code \"279\") (name \"unconnected-(NAVC2-VDD-Pad64)\") (class \"Default\") (node (ref \"NAVC2\") (pin \"64\") (pinfunction \"VDD\") (pintype \"power_in\")))"},
        {"source": "netlist", "quote": "(net (code \"10\") (name \"GND\") ... (node (ref \"FCC2\") (pin \"10\") (pinfunction \"VSS\") (pintype \"power_in\"))"}
      ],
      "impact": "With no ground or supply on the NAVC MCU, the navigation computer cannot power up or run, and the IMU, magnetometer, barometer and GPS it serves are lost.",
      "confidence": 0.35
    },
    {
      "category": "bridged_nets",
      "components": ["FCC2", "UART3_FCC2"],
      "pins": ["FCC2.48", "FCC2.73", "UART3_FCC2.3", "UART3_FCC2.4"],
      "nets": ["NET0017", "NET0094"],
      "observed": "Both signal pins of the UART3 connector are tied to the two STM32H753 VCAP pins (48 and 73). VCAP is a regulator-output pin that normally needs a 2.2 µF capacitor to ground. No UART3 peripheral pin is wired to the MCU.",
      "expected": "UART3 TX/RX should connect to the USART3 alternate-function pins, and VCAP pins should only connect to their stabilisation capacitors.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "netlist", "quote": "(net (code \"14\") (name \"NET0017\") ... (node (ref \"FCC2\") (pin \"48\") (pinfunction \"VCAP\") (pintype \"power_out\")) (node (ref \"UART3_FCC2\") (pin \"3\") ...))"},
        {"source": "netlist", "quote": "(net (code \"60\") (name \"NET0094\") ... (node (ref \"FCC2\") (pin \"73\") (pinfunction \"VCAP\") (pintype \"power_out\")) (node (ref \"UART3_FCC2\") (pin \"4\") ...))"}
      ],
      "impact": "UART3 is shorted to the core-supply regulator pins, so it cannot communicate. The VCAP regulator is also left without its required capacitor, which can destabilise the MCU core supply.",
      "confidence": 0.3
    },
    {
      "category": "wrong_value",
      "components": ["U15"],
      "pins": ["U15.1"],
      "nets": ["+3.3V"],
      "observed": "PCA9685 address pin A0 (pin 1) is tied to +3.3V, while A1 through A5 are on GND, giving I2C address 0x41.",
      "expected": "The specification lists the PCA9685 at I2C address 0x40, which requires A0 to be on GND. Its INA219 neighbour is also at 0x40 on the same bus, so the address choice should be confirmed.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "netlist", "quote": "(node (ref \"U15\") (pin \"1\") (pinfunction \"A0\") (pintype \"input\"))  [net +3.3V]"},
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "| PWM/servo driver | FCCPeripherals | NXP PCA9685PW (I2C 16-ch PWM), I2C addr 0x40 |"}
      ],
      "impact": "The firmware addressing the PWM driver at 0x40 would get no response, so the servo outputs would not be driven. Or, if the address was deliberately changed, the spec is out of date.",
      "confidence": 0.2
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The netlist shows the NAVC MCU's ground and supply pins unconnected, which would prevent the navigation computer from running. Two smaller candidates are the UART3 connector tied to the FCC's VCAP pins and an A0 address strap that conflicts with the spec. The first is the most likely true finding, but the design files are ambiguous enough that these could reflect reference-design artefacts."
}

